---
title: "How to use pi coding agent from your phone"
description: Follow and steer pi coding agent sessions from your phone while keeping the native pi TUI. Compares Pontia with do-it-yourself SSH and tmux, and pi in Termux, plus tmux key setup and safety notes.
published: "2026-11-11"
---

[pi](https://pi.dev) is a minimal terminal coding agent. Its sessions run in a terminal on your machine, so a long task is easy to start and hard to check on once you leave the desk. This article covers the ways to reach a pi session from a phone or another computer.

Pontia is the tool we build.

## The short answer

pi has no official remote or mobile client.

| Your situation | Best fit |
| --- | --- |
| You want to follow, steer, and continue pi sessions from a phone browser and keep the TUI | Pontia |
| You prefer to assemble and maintain the remote access yourself | SSH or Mosh, tmux, and a private network |
| You want to code on the phone itself, without another machine | pi in Termux on Android |

## Option 1: Pontia

Pontia is a free, open-source remote control for coding agents running on your own machine. You install it once, sign in, and open [app.pontia.dev](https://app.pontia.dev) on your phone. There is no VPN, port forwarding, or terminal app to set up.

Pontia keeps the native pi TUI. When you start a session from the Dashboard, Pontia launches pi in a tmux pane in the chosen project. A pi plugin reports the conversation to the Dashboard and accepts its controls. From a phone you can:

- start a pi session in a project directory and follow replies and tool output as they stream;
- send messages, queue them while pi works, or interrupt the current turn;
- edit an earlier message to branch the conversation from that point;
- change the model with `/model`, compact with `/compact`, and see estimated context usage;
- reference files with `@`.

The session is a real pi process, so you can also attach to it from a terminal at any time. Messages from the TUI and from the Dashboard go to the same conversation, and history stays in pi's own session files:

```sh
tmux ls                       # Pontia sessions start with pontia_
tmux attach -t SESSION_NAME
```

pi keeps running on your machine with its own provider credentials and configuration. The browser and your machine set up an end-to-end encrypted session through Pontia's relay, which only forwards ciphertext. Your machine connects out, so it needs no open inbound port.

Pontia supports Linux and macOS 11 or later, including headless servers. Beyond single sessions, [Workflows](/docs/workflows) run a large task as a sequence of pi sessions, each with a fresh context and explicit handoff files.

### Setup

Install `pi` and `tmux`, and configure your model provider in pi (`/login` and `/model`). Then run:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://get.pontia.dev/install.sh | sh
pontia init   # pi is selected by default
```

`pontia init` installs the pi plugin, starts the Pontia service, signs you in with Google or GitHub, and registers the machine with an official relay. On a headless machine, the sign-in prints a URL and a one-time code that you can approve from your phone. When it finishes, open [app.pontia.dev](https://app.pontia.dev), pick the machine, and start a session.

The [pi Agent Client reference](/docs/agent-clients/pi) covers custom pi commands and diagnostics. Advanced users can [run their own relay](/docs/self-hosted-edge) instead of the official one.

## Option 2: Build it yourself with SSH and tmux

You can also assemble remote access from standard tools: tmux keeps pi running, a private network such as Tailscale makes the machine reachable, and a phone SSH client connects to it.

```sh
# on the host
tmux new -s pi
pi

# from the phone, over SSH or Mosh
tmux attach -t pi
```

[Mosh](https://mosh.org/) keeps the connection alive when the phone switches networks, which plain SSH does not.

You set up and maintain each piece yourself: the private network on both devices, SSH keys on the phone, a terminal app, and tmux. On the phone you then use pi's full-screen TUI and keybindings on a small keyboard, and read long tool output by scrolling through tmux history.

## Option 3: Run pi on the phone itself

pi runs on Android through [Termux](https://termux.dev/). You install Node.js and pi with `npm`, then work in a folder on the phone. This suits small edits and scripts. The project files, toolchain, and builds then live on the phone, so it does not give you access to work on your main machine.

## Make pi keys work inside tmux

By default, tmux can report `Shift+Enter`, `Ctrl+Enter`, and `Enter` as the same key, so `Shift+Enter` submits your prompt instead of inserting a new line. This affects you whenever you use the pi TUI inside tmux, including Pontia sessions you attach to. pi's [tmux guide](https://pi.dev/docs/latest/tmux) recommends this `~/.tmux.conf` for tmux 3.5 or newer:

```tmux
set -g extended-keys on
set -g extended-keys-format csi-u
```

For tmux 3.2 through 3.4, use only `set -g extended-keys on`. The setting applies to the tmux server, so restart tmux before testing.

## Remote control does not make pi safer

pi does not stop to ask for approval before each tool call. Its [security guide](https://pi.dev/docs/latest/security) states that pi acts with the permissions of the account that started it, and that watching the transcript does not create a security boundary. That holds for every option here. Watching from a phone lets you interrupt a run that goes wrong, but it cannot stop a single command before it runs.

If you leave pi working unattended, limit what it can reach. Use a dedicated user account, a container or VM, and short-lived credentials scoped to the task. pi's guide to [running in an isolated environment](https://pi.dev/docs/latest/containerization) covers the options.

